# create-inside-try, narrowed (Task 9a): fires ONLY when the try's own
# finally block actually frees the just-constructed variable.
#
# P (genuine hazard -- rule fires): line 10 constructs SL as the first
# protected statement, and the SAME try's finally at line 13 frees SL. NOT on
# the correct pattern where the object is created before the try (line 15)
# and the try's first statement is SL.Add (line 16/17).
create-inside-try 10
!create-inside-try 15
!create-inside-try 16
!create-inside-try 17
#
# Q (YADF 5483 shape -- no finding): Sb is constructed at line 35 as the
# first statement of the OUTER try, but that try's finally (line 42) frees
# Root, not Sb -- Sb has its own nested try..finally (Sb.Free at line 39)
# immediately after. The outer finally never touches an undefined Sb.
!create-inside-try 35
#
# R (finally frees a different var -- no finding): A is constructed at line
# 55 as the first statement of the try, but the try's finally (line 58)
# frees B, a different, already-assigned variable -- not A.
!create-inside-try 55
